WHY THIS EXISTS

The firmware links ST's STM32 USB Device Library: usbd_core.c, usbd_ctlreq.c,
usbd_ioreq.c, usbd_cdc.c and the composite builder. ST licenses that library
under SLA0044, which permits use only on STMicroelectronics devices.

GPL version 3 section 7 forbids conveying a covered work under terms that impose
further restrictions on the rights it grants. A device restriction is such a
restriction. So without an explicit permission from JayECU's copyright holder,
conveying a firmware image that combines the two would be a conflict — not
because anyone is doing anything wrong, but because the two licences cannot both
be satisfied at once by a distributor who has not been given this permission.

This file is that permission, and it can only come from the copyright holder,
which is why it is a separate document and not a note in a source file.

Nothing here alters ST's terms. Their files remain under SLA0044, and the device
restriction still binds anyone using them. What this permission does is let the
COMBINATION be distributed under the GPL — which is the only thing that was in
doubt.

The practical effect is none, because the firmware targets an STM32F767 and no
other part will run it. The exception exists so that the licence describes what
is actually happening, rather than leaving a conflict for someone else to find.
